<% content_for :title, "Localisation — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "Every piece of text an installed component shows goes through I18n with an English default. The full list of keys, the ones JavaScript reads, and how to override them." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Localisation",
        description: "Installed components look up their text in I18n and fall back to English. If your app defines a key, the component uses your text instead." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <p class="leading-7">
      Any text a component renders by itself, such as a close button's label, a
      pagination arrow or an empty table's message, goes through
      <%= docs_code("I18n.t") %> with the English text as the default:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:call], language: :ruby) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Because of that default, a component works right after you install it: with no
      locale file, it renders in English. You don't have to configure anything first,
      and apps that don't use I18n at all are not affected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Overriding a string") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Define the key in your own locale file. You don't need to register anything or
      pass an argument to the component; the key is all there is to it.
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:override], language: :yaml) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Only the keys you define change. The rest keep their English defaults, so you
      can translate one component at a time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("The keys") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Every key a component reads, with its default text. Keys marked
      <strong>value</strong> are written into a <%= docs_code("data-*-value") %>
      attribute and used by a Stimulus controller instead of being printed by the
      server; the next section explains why that matters.
    </p>

    <% @keys.each do |component, entries| %>
      <h3 class="scroll-m-20 pt-4 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight"><%= docs_code(component) %></h3>
      <%= render "docs/table",
            headers: [ "Key", "Default", "Kind" ],
            rows: entries.map { |entry|
              [
                docs_code(entry[:key]),
                entry[:default],
                entry[:stimulus] ? "value" : "server"
              ]
            } %>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Five of them are read by JavaScript") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("ui.data_table.selection") %>, <%= docs_code("ui.data_table.page") %>,
      <%= docs_code("ui.calendar.month") %>, <%= docs_code("ui.calendar.year") %> and
      <%= docs_code("ui.sonner.close") %> are not
      printed on the page. They are looked up once, when the component renders, and
      written into a <%= docs_code("data-*-value") %> attribute for the controller to
      read. You translate them the same way as the others, since they are ordinary
      I18n keys.
    </p>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Two of them need care. The data table's selection count and page counter are
      filled in <em>by the controller</em>, not by I18n, so your translation has to
      keep their placeholders:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:interpolated], language: :yaml) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      If you translate or remove <%= docs_code("%{selected}") %>, <%= docs_code("%{page}") %>
      or <%= docs_code("%{total}") %>, the caption shows the placeholder as text or no
      number at all, and you won't get an error, because I18n never sees these
      placeholders.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Month and day names come from Rails") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The calendar's caption and column headers are not in the
      <%= docs_code("ui.") %> namespace. The calendar reads them from Rails' own date
      translations, <%= docs_code("date.month_names") %> and
      <%= docs_code("date.abbr_day_names") %>. They are in English by default, and in
      your language once your app loads the translations, either from
      <%= link_to "rails-i18n", "https://github.com/svenfuchs/rails-i18n", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>
      or from your own locale file. To use different names in one calendar, pass them in:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:calendar_names]) %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      You need the <%= docs_code(".compact") %>: Rails'
      <%= docs_code("date.abbr_month_names") %> starts at index 1, so its first element
      is <%= docs_code("nil") %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("A worked example") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      This site is one. Its Portuguese pages render the same components you install,
      and the site defines the whole <%= docs_code("ui.") %> block once. That is why
      the text the components add themselves on <%= docs_code("/pt") %> (close
      buttons, pagination arrows, the empty table message) is in Portuguese.
    </p>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= link_to "sandbox/config/locales/pt.yml",
            "https://github.com/edumoraes/shadwire/blob/main/sandbox/config/locales/pt.yml",
            target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>
      has every key on this page, and you can copy it as a starting point for an app
      in Portuguese.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
